WITH ins AS (INSERT INTO sais_resource_deadlines (resource_id, appeal_deadline, continued_benefits_deadline, hearing_request_deadline, reconsideration_deadline, judicial_review_deadline, source_file, source_hash8) VALUES 
('SAIS-HI-010', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. State malpractice limitations periods apply, commonly 1-3 years from injury or discovery, with statutes of repose in many states and different tolling rules for minors and incapacitated persons.', 'Send a written records-preservation and litigation-hold letter to the facility immediately, naming the medical record, the audit trail, staffing and assignment records, and the policies in effect. Staffing records are retained on shorter schedules than medical records.', 'Pre-suit notice of intent where required: commonly 90 days. Facility complaint to the state survey agency: file at any time, and an on-site survey triggered by a complaint produces public findings usable in a civil matter.', 'Certificate or affidavit of merit where required: commonly due with the complaint or within 60-90 days. Many states require the reviewing expert to be a nurse in the same practice area rather than a physician.', 'For federally certified facilities, CMS survey findings and enforcement actions are public and searchable. Prior deficiencies on the same standard establish notice at the facility level.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-011', 'Insurer denial or downcoding: standard internal appeal deadlines apply, generally 180 days for ERISA plans. Appeal the level-of-service determination separately from the coverage determination.', 'Request that the provider hold the account while the insurance appeal and any hardship application are pending. Get the hold in writing and confirm it before any collection referral date stated on the bill.', 'No Surprises Act patient-provider dispute resolution for uninsured or self-pay patients billed substantially above a good faith estimate: generally 120 days from the bill. Applies to air ambulance and to other covered items, generally not to ground ambulance.', 'Request the run report and the itemized bill and dispute specific line items -- mileage, level of service, and emergency versus non-emergency designation are the three most frequently misbilled elements.', 'State insurance department complaint: file at any time and in parallel. Where a state ground ambulance protection statute exists, the state complaint is the operative route rather than the federal one.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-012', 'ERISA and ACA plans: 180 days for the internal appeal. Medicare Advantage reconsideration: generally 60 days from the denial notice. Medicaid managed care appeal: commonly 60 days from the notice of adverse benefit determination -- verify your state.', 'MEDICAID CONTINUATION OF BENEFITS: where an existing authorized service is being reduced or terminated, requesting a fair hearing within the state''s continuation window -- commonly 10 DAYS from the notice -- generally continues the service pending the hearing. This is one of the shortest and most valuable deadlines in health coverage.', 'EXPEDITED review: request by name where delay would seriously jeopardize health. Generally decided within 72 hours. Medicare Part D expedited coverage determination: 24 hours. Medicare fast-track appeals of service terminations can be as short as noon the day after notice.', 'Peer-to-peer review: request immediately through the ordering clinician, before or alongside the formal appeal. It is the fastest reversal route and does not consume the formal appeal clock.', 'External review generally 4 months from final internal denial. Medicare has a five-level appeal ladder with distinct deadlines at each level -- generally 120 days for redetermination, then 180 days for reconsideration, then 60 days at each subsequent level.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-001', 'ERISA health plans: 180 days from the denial notice to file the internal appeal. Non-ERISA and property/casualty lines: set by policy and state law, commonly 30-180 days -- read the denial letter for the stated deadline and treat it as real.', 'For ongoing/concurrent care that is already being provided, request continued coverage AT THE SAME TIME you appeal -- if you appeal before the existing authorization ends, coverage generally continues during the appeal. Filing one day after termination usually forfeits this.', 'External review request: generally 4 months from the final internal denial. Expedited external review is available where delay would seriously jeopardize health -- ask for it explicitly by name.', 'Many insurers permit an informal reconsideration before formal appeal. Do not let a reconsideration request consume the formal appeal clock -- file both, and say in writing that the formal appeal is preserved.', 'ERISA suit: file after exhausting the plan''s internal appeals. Contractual suit-limitation clauses in non-ERISA policies commonly cut this to 1-2 years from the loss -- check the policy, not the statute.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-002', 'Eviction judgment appeal: extremely short in most states, commonly 5-30 days from judgment, and in many jurisdictions an appeal bond or ongoing rent deposit is required to stay the writ. Ask the court clerk the exact number the day judgment enters.', 'If a writ of possession has issued, ask immediately about a stay of execution pending appeal and about any statutory right to redeem the tenancy by paying the judgment amount -- both are time-limited and both are commonly missed.', 'You must appear on the return date stated in the summons -- failing to appear generally produces an immediate default judgment for possession. This is the single most consequential deadline in housing law. Appearing and asking for a continuance preserves far more than not appearing.', 'Motion to set aside a default eviction judgment: state-specific and short, commonly 10-30 days. Move the moment you learn of the default -- delay is itself a ground for denial.', 'HUD fair housing complaint: 1 year from the last discriminatory act. Private Fair Housing Act lawsuit: 2 years from the last discriminatory act. These run separately -- filing with HUD does not extend the 2-year court deadline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-003', 'Not an administrative appeal posture -- the operative clock is the statute of limitations, commonly 1-3 years from the injury or from the date the injury was or reasonably should have been discovered. Statutes of repose in many states cut off claims entirely at a fixed outer date regardless of discovery.', 'Not applicable in the benefits sense. The functional equivalent is preserving evidence: send a written litigation-hold/records-preservation letter to the provider and facility as early as possible, which puts them on notice not to destroy or alter records.', 'Pre-suit notice of intent, where required: commonly 90 days before filing suit, and in most states it tolls the limitations period while it runs. Medical review panel submission, where required: state-specific and mandatory before a court will hear the case.', 'Certificate/affidavit of merit: commonly due with the complaint or within 60-90 days after filing. Some states permit one extension for good cause; many do not.', 'Minors and incapacitated plaintiffs are subject to different tolling rules in nearly every state, sometimes extending the deadline for years. Verify the rule for the injured person''s specific status -- do not assume the adult deadline applies.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-004', 'EEOC charge: 180 days from the discriminatory act, or 300 days where a state or local fair employment agency has concurrent jurisdiction. Federal employees follow a different and much shorter track -- 45 days to contact an agency EEO counselor.', 'If you are still employed, document contemporaneously and preserve access to your own personnel records, performance reviews, and communications BEFORE any separation -- access is routinely cut off the day of termination. Requesting your personnel file is a right in many states.', 'Request for hearing before an EEOC administrative judge (federal-sector complaints): 30 days from receipt of the agency''s final action or from the close of the investigation period.', 'EEOC reconsideration of a dismissal or of an Office of Federal Operations decision: 30 days. State agency reconsideration deadlines vary and are commonly 15-30 days.', 'AFTER RIGHT-TO-SUE: 90 DAYS TO FILE IN COURT. This is the hardest deadline in employment law and the most commonly blown. Do not wait for an attorney to be retained before docketing this date.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-005', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. The operative clock is the state UDAP statute of limitations, commonly 1-6 years depending on the state, frequently running from discovery of the deception rather than from the transaction.', 'If payments are ongoing, act on the payment channel immediately and separately from the fraud claim: credit card chargeback rights under the Fair Credit Billing Act generally require written notice within 60 days of the statement, and revoking an ACH authorization is a distinct written step.', 'Small claims filing where the amount is within the jurisdictional limit -- no attorney required, filing fees are low, and many state UDAP statutes apply in small claims court with their damages multipliers intact.', 'State AG complaint: no deadline to file, but file early -- AG offices act on volume and pattern, and an early complaint is what builds the pattern for everyone who comes after you.', 'TILA rescission on dwelling-secured loans: 3 business days from closing, extended to 3 years where required material disclosures were not properly delivered. Verify the extended right before assuming the window closed.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-006', 'Internal appeal or reconsideration deadlines are set by policy and state law, commonly 30-60 days from the coverage determination. The denial letter must state the deadline -- if it does not, that omission is itself a complaint ground with the state regulator.', 'For an ongoing rental-car or loss-of-use benefit that the insurer is terminating, dispute the termination in writing BEFORE the stated end date -- retroactive reinstatement is materially harder than preventing the cutoff.', 'Invoke the appraisal clause in writing to dispute the AMOUNT of a loss. Note the distinction: appraisal resolves valuation disputes, not coverage disputes -- if the insurer denies that the loss is covered at all, appraisal is the wrong tool and a regulator complaint or suit is the right one.', 'State Department of Insurance complaint: file at any time, and file in parallel with the internal dispute rather than after it. Regulator complaints are separately docketed and generate a written carrier response.', 'Contractual suit-limitation clauses commonly require suit within 1-2 years of the loss, which is often SHORTER than the state''s general contract limitations period. The policy controls -- read it.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-007', 'Internal appeal/reconsideration of a denial or underpayment: policy- and state-specific, commonly 30-60 days. NFIP flood claims: 60 days from the written denial to appeal to FEMA.', 'Additional living expense (ALE) or loss-of-use coverage the insurer is terminating: dispute in writing BEFORE the termination date, and cite the policy''s stated ALE period. Once you have vacated the coverage, restoring it is much harder.', 'Appraisal clause: either party may demand binding appraisal of the amount of loss. This is the principal tool for a scope dispute and is generally faster and cheaper than suit. It does not resolve coverage denials.', 'Sworn proof of loss: commonly due within 60 days of the insurer''s request. Any extension must be obtained in writing before the deadline passes, not after.', 'Suit-limitation clause: commonly 1-2 years from the date of loss, and shorter than the general contract statute in most states. NFIP: 1 year from the written denial to file in federal court -- this is strict and is not extended by ongoing negotiation.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-008', 'Internal appeal of a beneficiary denial: policy- and state-specific, commonly 30-180 days from the denial letter. ERISA-governed group life through an employer: generally 180 days, and ERISA procedural protections apply including the right to the full claim file.', 'For a policy the insurer says has lapsed, request reinstatement IMMEDIATELY and in writing -- reinstatement rights are usually time-limited (commonly 3-5 years from lapse) and may require evidence of insurability that becomes harder to supply with time.', 'State Department of Insurance complaint: file at any time. For contested-beneficiary situations where the insurer files an interpleader action in court, the deadline to answer is set by the summons and is typically 20-30 days -- do not ignore interpleader papers.', 'Request the complete underwriting file and the application as submitted. Where an agent completed the application, discrepancies between what the insured said and what the agent wrote are a recognized and frequently successful ground for reconsideration.', 'Suit-limitation clauses and state contract statutes of limitation commonly permit 2-6 years. ERISA-governed group life requires exhaustion of plan appeals before suit.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-009', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. The operative clock is the state statute of limitations for personal injury or property damage, commonly 1-6 years, frequently running from discovery of the injury and its cause rather than from purchase.', 'Statutes of repose in many states bar product claims entirely after a fixed number of years from first sale (commonly 6-15 years) regardless of when the injury occurred. Check this before assuming a claim survives -- it is a hard cutoff, not a discovery-based one.', 'CPSC/NHTSA/FDA report: file immediately, no deadline. Searching the existing report database for the same model FIRST is the fastest way to establish that the manufacturer had prior notice.', 'If a recall exists, remedy claims under the recall generally have their own deadlines set by the recall notice. A recall remedy does not waive an injury claim -- accepting a repair does not settle a personal injury case unless you sign a release.', 'Multidistrict litigation and class settlements carry their own claim deadlines and opt-out windows, commonly 30-90 days from notice. Opting out preserves an individual claim that is frequently worth far more than the class share for a seriously injured person.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-010', 'FCRA dispute reinvestigation: the credit reporting agency generally has 30 days to complete it, extended to 45 if you supply additional information during the period. Blocking under the identity theft provision is faster -- 4 business days.', 'A security freeze must be placed separately at each nationwide credit reporting agency and is free under federal law. It stays in place until you lift it. Place it BEFORE disputing, so no new accounts open during the reinvestigation window.', 'Request the thief''s application and transaction records from each business that opened an account. The business must generally provide them within 30 days of your request accompanied by the identity theft report and proof of identity.', 'If a bureau rejects a block, it must notify you and state why -- and you may resubmit with corrected documentation. A rejected block is not the end; it is usually a documentation defect.', 'FCRA private lawsuit: generally the earlier of 2 years from discovery of the violation or 5 years from the violation itself. Willful violations carry statutory damages without proof of actual loss.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-011', 'FDCPA private lawsuit: 1 YEAR from the date of the violation. This is one of the shortest federal consumer limitations periods and it is missed constantly. Each separate violation may start its own clock.', 'If a lawsuit has been filed against you, you must ANSWER by the deadline on the summons (commonly 20-30 days). Default judgments are how the overwhelming majority of collection suits end, and a default converts a possibly time-barred or undocumented debt into an enforceable judgment with wage garnishment attached.', 'Written dispute and validation demand: within 30 DAYS of the validation notice. After 30 days you may still dispute, but the automatic obligation to cease collection pending verification is lost.', 'Motion to vacate a default judgment: state-specific and short, commonly 30 days to 1 year. Improper service -- which is common in collection practice -- is a recognized ground and is worth investigating before assuming the judgment is final.', 'Check the state statute of limitations on the underlying debt (commonly 3-6 years). Suing on a time-barred debt is itself an FDCPA violation in most circuits, but the defense must be RAISED -- a court will not raise it for you, and making a payment can restart the clock in some states.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-012', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. State lemon law eligibility windows commonly run 1-2 years from delivery or 12,000-24,000 miles, whichever comes first -- and the qualifying repair attempts must generally occur inside that window.', 'Keep the vehicle in service and keep documenting. Ceasing to drive or trading the vehicle in before the claim resolves generally destroys the remedy, since the statutory remedy is replacement or repurchase of the vehicle you still own.', 'Final written notice to the manufacturer with a last opportunity to repair: required in most states before the presumption applies. Send it certified with return receipt and keep the receipt -- this is the step manufacturers most often argue was never completed.', 'State-run or manufacturer-sponsored arbitration: filing deadlines are program-specific, commonly within the eligibility window plus a short grace period. A consumer who rejects a non-binding arbitration award generally retains the right to sue.', 'Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act claim: governed by the state''s breach-of-warranty limitations period, commonly 4 years under UCC Article 2 from tender of delivery. This often survives after the state lemon law window has closed -- a critical fallback.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-013', 'Statute of limitations: commonly 1-6 years by state and theory, from injury or from discovery where a discovery rule applies. Wrongful death is frequently governed by a separate and often shorter period running from the date of death.', 'Preserve evidence immediately: photographs of the scene and injuries, the names and contact information of every witness, and a written preservation demand for any surveillance video. Commercial video is routinely overwritten in 7-30 days and is gone permanently after that.', 'GOVERNMENT NOTICE OF CLAIM: commonly 30-180 days where any defendant is a public entity, and it is a precondition to suit rather than a formality. Federal Tort Claims Act: SF-95 administrative claim within 2 years of accrual.', 'Federal Tort Claims Act: after the agency denies the administrative claim, you have 6 MONTHS to file in federal district court. State tort claims acts have their own analogous post-denial windows.', 'Minors and legally incapacitated claimants are subject to tolling in nearly every state, frequently extending the deadline until some period after majority. Verify the rule for the injured person''s status rather than applying the adult deadline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-014', 'Insurer denial or underpayment: internal appeal deadlines are policy- and state-specific, commonly 30-60 days. Third-party negligence claims run on the state property damage statute of limitations, commonly 2-6 years.', 'Mitigation is generally mandatory -- take reasonable steps to prevent further damage and keep every receipt. Mitigation costs are usually recoverable, and failure to mitigate is a standard and effective defense to the additional damage that results.', 'Appraisal clause where an insurance policy is involved: either party may demand binding appraisal of the amount of loss. Notice-of-claim deadlines apply where a public entity is potentially responsible -- commonly 30-180 days.', 'Request the adjuster''s complete estimate and scope sheet in writing and respond line by line. A line-item rebuttal supported by an independent estimate is the most effective reconsideration instrument in property claims.', 'Contractual suit-limitation clauses in property policies commonly require suit within 1-2 years of the loss, shorter than the general statute. The policy controls over the statute.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-015', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. Written contract statute of limitations is commonly 4-6 years; oral contracts commonly 2-4 years; UCC sale-of-goods claims commonly 4 years from tender of delivery. State-specific -- verify.', 'Mitigation is required. Continue performing your own obligations unless you have a clear legal right to suspend, and document every step taken to limit the loss -- unmitigated damages are generally not recoverable regardless of who breached.', 'Contractual notice-and-cure: send written notice within any period the contract specifies and allow the full cure period before terminating. Where the contract is silent, send written notice anyway -- it establishes the breach date and forecloses a later claim of surprise.', 'Demand letter with a specific deadline for response: not legally required in most cases but practically decisive, and required by statute in some states before certain claims (including many UDAP claims) may be filed.', 'Mandatory arbitration clauses have their own filing deadlines and rules. Note that many consumer arbitration clauses require the drafting company to pay most fees -- verify before assuming arbitration is unaffordable.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-016', 'Appeal from a small claims judgment: short and state-specific, commonly 10-30 days from entry of judgment. Some states allow a full new trial (trial de novo) on appeal; others review only for legal error -- these are very different postures.', 'If you are the defendant and a default judgment has already entered, move to vacate immediately. Grounds commonly include defective service and excusable neglect, and the window is short -- often 30 days, sometimes up to a year.', 'You must appear on the hearing date stated in the notice. Non-appearance by a plaintiff generally results in dismissal; non-appearance by a defendant generally results in a default judgment. Continuances must usually be requested in advance and in writing.', 'Post-judgment motions to correct or amend: state-specific, commonly 10-30 days. A clerical error in the judgment amount or party name should be corrected before you attempt collection, since it will block enforcement.', 'Judgments are enforceable for a defined term -- commonly 5-20 years -- and are generally renewable before expiration. Docket the renewal deadline the day you win; an expired unrenewed judgment is uncollectible.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-017', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. UCC breach of warranty: commonly 4 years from tender of delivery, though a warranty explicitly extending to future performance may run from discovery of the breach instead.', 'Stop using the product only if continued use would worsen the defect or create a safety risk; otherwise continue normal use and document. Preserve the product, the warranty document, the receipt, and every repair record.', 'Where the written warranty requires an informal dispute settlement mechanism, submit to it before filing suit under Magnuson-Moss. The program must meet FTC rule requirements and its decision cannot bind you.', 'Send a written demand identifying the specific warranty provision breached and the remedy sought. Where the seller has had a reasonable number of repair attempts and the defect persists, state explicitly that the limited remedy has failed of its essential purpose -- that phrase unlocks the full range of UCC remedies notwithstanding a repair-or-replace limitation.', 'Magnuson-Moss claims are brought in state or federal court under the state''s warranty limitations period, commonly 4 years. Federal court has a jurisdictional amount threshold that most consumer claims do not meet, so state court is the normal forum.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PC-018', 'Utility internal appeal: commonly 10-30 days from the disputed bill or notice. State commission informal complaint: file at any time, and file BEFORE the disconnection date stated on the notice.', 'FILING THE DISPUTE IS THE CONTINUED-SERVICE MECHANISM -- in most states a pending formal dispute or commission complaint stays disconnection on the disputed amount. You generally must continue paying the undisputed portion to keep the protection.', 'Formal evidentiary hearing before the state commission: request within the period stated in the informal complaint decision, commonly 10-30 days. Medical certification must be filed BEFORE the disconnection date on the notice to prevent shutoff rather than to seek restoration.', 'Request reconsideration of a commission staff decision within the period stated in the decision, commonly 10-30 days. Back-billing for a meter or billing error is limited in most states to a defined look-back period -- raise that limit explicitly.', 'Appeal of a final commission order to state court: commonly 30 days from the order, and generally requires that the issue was preserved at the commission level. Raise every argument at the agency or lose it.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-001-01', 'Not an appeal posture. Where an agency refuses to take a report, escalate immediately to a supervisor, the state missing persons clearinghouse, and for anyone under 21 to NCMEC at 800-843-5678, which routinely intervenes with agencies.', 'The case remains active in NCIC until cancelled. Ask the agency to confirm the entry and to keep it current. Records that are entered but never updated are a recurring failure point.', 'NCIC ENTRY FOR ANYONE UNDER 21: required within TWO HOURS of the report under federal law. For adults, entry is discretionary in some states -- ask explicitly for NCIC entry and escalate if it is refused.', 'If the report is taken but classified as a voluntary absence or runaway and you disagree, ask in writing that the classification be reconsidered and state your reasons. Classification drives resource allocation.', 'Federal law also requires reporting of missing children to NCMEC and prohibits removing a case from the database solely because of the child''s age. For adults, entry generally persists until resolved.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-001-02', 'Not an appeal posture. Where an agency declines to enter or update a record, escalate to the state clearinghouse and, for anyone under 21, to NCMEC, which intervenes with agencies as a matter of routine.', 'Keep the record CURRENT. New photographs, new identifying details, dental and medical records, and DNA reference samples should be added as they become available -- stale records are matched less often.', 'Provide a DNA REFERENCE SAMPLE through NamUs or law enforcement as early as possible. Family reference DNA is what enables comparison against unidentified remains, and it is free.', 'Ask specifically whether DENTAL RECORDS and FINGERPRINTS have been obtained and entered. These are the two most frequently missing elements in unresolved cases and both are free to add.', 'NamUs records persist indefinitely and are continuously cross-compared. Cases decades old are resolved through this mechanism regularly, which is why entering a record matters even long after the disappearance.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-001-03', 'Not an appeal posture. Where an alert is declined, ask which criterion was not met and supply the missing fact -- alert decisions are frequently reversed when the danger element is documented.', 'Alerts are most effective in the first hours. A declined alert does not preclude a later one if circumstances change or new information emerges -- ask again when they do.', 'Request the alert at the time of the initial report. Ask which alert types your state operates and which one fits -- most families do not know Silver, Ashanti, and endangered advisories exist.', 'If law enforcement declines, ask the STATE CLEARINGHOUSE directly. Clearinghouses can frequently issue or push for advisories that a local agency did not pursue.', 'Not applicable. Alert systems run in parallel with database entry and investigation and consume no other deadline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-001-04', 'Not an appeal posture. Evidence lost to retention expiry is generally unrecoverable -- there is no later remedy for data that has aged out.', 'PRESERVE THE PERSON''S DEVICES AND ROOM WITHOUT DISTURBING THEM. Logging into accounts, wiping a phone, or cleaning a vehicle destroys evidence and can also destroy the ability to establish a timeline.', 'Ask for carrier PING and exigent location requests IMMEDIATELY. This is measured in hours, not days, and depends on the phone still being powered and on the request being made.', 'If the agency has not sent preservation letters, ask again in writing and copy the state clearinghouse. Document the request and the date -- an unpreserved record is a recurring cause of case failure.', 'Surveillance video: typically overwritten in 7-30 DAYS. Families may send their own written preservation requests to businesses and neighbors in addition to law enforcement collection.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-001-05', 'Not an appeal posture. Where an agency will not engage, escalate laterally to the clearinghouse and the FBI rather than waiting -- their involvement does not require the local agency''s consent.', 'Keep the NCIC and NamUs records ACTIVE and CURRENT. Ask the agency annually to confirm the entry has not been cancelled and to add any new identifying information.', 'Request a formal case review or a cold case unit referral in writing after a defined period without progress. Many agencies and states have cold case units and review protocols that are not offered unprompted.', 'Where the agency declines a review, request the declination in writing and forward it to the state clearinghouse and the FBI field office. A documented refusal frequently produces movement.', 'There is no statute of limitations on a missing person case. NamUs cross-comparison resolves cases decades later, which is why maintaining a complete and current record matters indefinitely.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-001-06', 'Denial of a benefit or compensation claim: administrative appeal, commonly 30 days. Court decisions on absentee conservatorship or declaration of death: commonly 30 days to appeal.', 'Where the missing person''s income supported the household, act early on housing, utilities, and benefits -- eviction and shutoff protections and hardship programs are far easier to invoke before arrears accumulate.', 'Petition for absentee conservatorship or protective arrangement: no fixed deadline, but bills, mortgages, and accounts do not pause. Ask the probate clerk what mechanism your state provides.', 'Declaration of death in absentia: commonly available after roughly seven years, substantially shorter where the person disappeared in circumstances of specific peril. Verify your state''s rule rather than assuming the seven-year figure.', 'Life insurance and Social Security survivor claims generally follow a declaration of death and have their own filing procedures and appeal deadlines once the declaration issues.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-002-01', 'Not an appeal posture. Shelter admission decisions are program-level; if one program cannot take you, ask them to call others and to check other counties -- programs coordinate and will make the calls for you.', 'If you are turned away for capacity, ASK FOR A HOTEL VOUCHER AND FOR PLACEMENT SEARCH ASSISTANCE explicitly. Being told ''we are full'' is frequently the beginning of the conversation rather than the end of it.', 'Coordinated entry assessment through your local Continuum of Care determines access to most homeless and rapid re-housing assistance. Ask the DV program to complete it with you -- DV survivors generally have a confidential intake path that does not enter the shared data system.', 'If a program declines and you believe it was because of a protected characteristic, a disability, a service animal, or having a son over a certain age, ask for the reason in writing. Federal nondiscrimination rules apply to funded programs.', 'Not applicable. Shelter and advocacy run in parallel with every legal track in this dossier and consume none of their deadlines.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18')
ON CONFLICT (resource_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING resource_id) SELECT count(*) AS inserted FROM ins